INT. COURTHOUSE ELEVATOR, FOURTH FLOOR - DAY

A dented steel box with a cracked mirror and a laminated placard: MAX OCCUPANCY 8. The numbers above the door flicker.

DEPUTY DISTRICT ATTORNEY MARGUERITE OKWUOSA-HALE (40s, charcoal suit, case files in both arms) stands in one corner. In the other corner, PUBLIC DEFENDER TABITHA NGUYEN-FELLOWES (30s, rumpled blazer, coffee) leans against the rail.

The elevator shudders and stops between floors. The lights dim. A tiny speaker coughs.

MAINTENANCE (V.O.)
(through the speaker)
Stuck on four. Hang tight, folks.

Marguerite sighs. Tabitha sips her coffee.

MARGUERITE
Ms. Nguyen-Fellowes.

TABITHA
Ms. Okwuosa-Hale.

MARGUERITE
You filed a motion to suppress at six this morning.

TABITHA
I did.

MARGUERITE
You're supposed to file by five.

TABITHA
The email server was down.

MARGUERITE
That's not my problem.

TABITHA
It will be when the judge reads it.

MARGUERITE
She'll deny it.

TABITHA
She'll hear it. She'll rule on the merits.

MARGUERITE
Tabitha.

TABITHA
Marguerite.

MARGUERITE
Your client's own phone placed him at the scene. You don't have the merits.

TABITHA
My client's phone was seized without a warrant.

MARGUERITE
With consent.

TABITHA
From his sister. Who doesn't live there.

Marguerite's arms tighten around her files. A fluorescent tube flickers. She sets the files on the floor and kicks them into the corner.

MARGUERITE
(muttering)
She lives there.

TABITHA
What?

MARGUERITE
Nothing.

TABITHA
She said she moved out in June.

MARGUERITE
She said a lot of things.

TABITHA
Marguerite. Is there a lease?

MARGUERITE
That is not for me to discuss in a stuck elevator.

TABITHA
Then discuss it in the courtroom.

MARGUERITE
I will.

TABITHA
Good.

The elevator lurches. Both of them grab the rail. The doors grind open onto the fifth floor. Between them: a bare corridor, and light.

MAINTENANCE (V.O.)
(through the speaker)
Okay, you're good.

Marguerite picks up her files. Tabitha steps out first.

TABITHA
After you.

MARGUERITE
You're holding the door.

TABITHA
Habit.

MARGUERITE
I'm asking you to hold it.

Tabitha holds it. Marguerite steps through, stops, and for the first time looks at her directly.

MARGUERITE (CONT'D)
There's a lease. It's in the evidence box. Item thirty-one.

TABITHA
You're telling me?

MARGUERITE
I'm telling the court. Shortly. You may as well know first.

She walks down the corridor. Tabitha lets the door close behind her and watches her go.
